      "writtenOn": "2026-10-28T22:48:52Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The locking looks right to me: the folio lock is taken before the PTLs, the wait happens with the PTEs unmapped, and `swp_swapcount()` runs under the PTLs. But I think most of this function repeats the swap branch of `move_pages_ptes()` and `move_swap_pte()`: `get_swap_device()`, the swap cache lookup, the folio lock and its retry, the `pmd_same()`/`pte_same()` recheck, the `SWAP_HAS_CACHE` check and the PTE move. Only the choice of which PTEs move with the entry is specific to 4KB processes.\n\nWould it work to keep the generic path, let non-exclusive swap PTEs of 4KB VMAs through its `pte_swp_exclusive()` check, and have `move_swap_pte()` ask a helper in `mm/p3s/anon.h`, next to `p3s_uffd_move_nr_slices()`, how many PTEs to move? Under both locks, it could count the PTEs from `src_pte` within the window and the range that hold `entry`, as `p3s_swapin_free_sibling_swap()` does with `vma_folio_slice_bounds()`. It would return that count only if it equals `swp_swapcount(entry)`, the destination PTEs are none, and the swap cache folio, if any, is unmapped and unpinned.\n\nThat would leave only a few lines in `mm/userfaultfd.c`, where aosp/4367997 adds another ~230, and avoid a second copy of the lock and retry handling. It would also move windows that were only partly mapped at swap-out. Here, a none PTE in the window makes the code fall back to the generic path, which still returns `-EBUSY`. If that doesn\u0027t work out, could this function at least move to `mm/p3s/anon.h` as a `p3s_uffd_*()` helper, with a one-line call here?",
      "isAi": true,
      "revId": "a19600eed357ed5fd1784a877ec41b0a2c5c72a2",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
